  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through the end of October.\n\nLarge scale ridging persists at the surface and aloft through\nthe end of the week. This results in dry weather and above\nnormal temperatures through the end of October.\n\nSurface high pressure of Canadian origin that built over the \nMid-Atlantic this past weekend will drift south and west across \nthe central Appalachians and Tennessee Valley through tonight. \nThis continues a gradual warming trend that began on Sunday.\n\nA weakening cold front will approach from the north tonight, \ntied to low pressure over the Canadian Maritimes. But without \nmuch forcing or moisture, no precipitation is expected. The \nfront will cross the region Tuesday morning, then stall and \nweaken, or perhaps lift northward as a weak warm front, through\nmid week. Warmer than normal temperatures will continue during \nthis time as a result.\n\nAnother large area of high pressure will build to the north for\nthe second half of the week. The position of the high should\nallow for southeast to southwest winds and continued warm\ntemperatures by late October standards. The warmest day\ncurrently appears to be Friday when highs in the mid to upper\n70s are possible.\n\nSaturday may be a few degrees cooler as clouds begin to increase\nfrom the west ahead of a trough developing over the middle of\nthe country. Highs will likely still be above normal into the\nlower 70s, however.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nTroughing developing over the western CONUS late this week looks\nto eject one or more pieces of shortwave energy this weekend. \nThis results in lowering heights and an approaching surface \nfrontal system as the calendar flips to November. There is a lot\nof timing spread in the guidance on when these waves approach \nour area, but some low precipitation chances do return to the \nforecast by the end of the upcoming weekend.",
